2.1 Any real-time language must enable (1) delay — suspending a task until it's time to continue, and (2) deadline — indicating that a point in the code must be reached no later than a specified time. (More broadly: supporting both time-triggered periodic activities and event-triggered sporadic/aperiodic activities.)

2.2 Because a real-time system interacts with a physical environment. Logical correctness alone isn't enough — a correct output delivered too late (or too early) is still a failure. The physical process imposes timing constraints (sampling rates, actuation deadlines), so the system must be correct in both the functional and temporal domain.

2.3

Verify requirements/designs — assuming an infinitely fast, reliable computer, check that the temporal requirements are coherent and consistent (can they be satisfied at all?).
Verify the implementation — with a finite set of (possibly unreliable) hardware resources, check that the temporal requirements can actually be satisfied.

2.4 A temporal scope is a collection of statements with an associated timing constraint. It's introduced to make it easier to specify the various timing constraints in real-time applications. Its attributes include deadline, minimum delay, maximum execution time, and maximum elapse time.

2.5

Input jitter: a bound on the variation in when sensor readings are taken — e.g. a 20 ms loop requiring inputs every 20 ms ± 2 ms ("read from sensors within 2ms").
Output jitter: a bound preventing the output from being produced too early as well as too late — e.g. output required by 15 ms but not before 12 ms.

2.6 A periodic event/task occurs at a fixed, regular interval (period T), e.g. a control loop every 20 ms. An aperiodic event has no minimum arrival interval — it can occur at any time, unpredictably, so it must be constrained by other means (e.g. a CPU budget like 10 ms per 50 ms) and is run when possible. (A sporadic event is the middle case: irregular, but with a guaranteed minimum inter-arrival time.)

2.7 In Figure 1 (time flows downward from "Now"):

Minimum delay — the gap from "Now" down to the top of the first execution unit (the time that must elapse before the scope starts). In the slide version this is arrow (2) at the top.
Deadline — the long vertical line ending at the bottom of the figure (the time by which execution must be finished), arrow (1).
Maximum elapse time — the span from the start of the first execution unit to the end of the last one (total wall-clock time including gaps), arrow (4).
Maximum execution time — a + b + c, the sum of the three execution units (actual CPU time only), label (3).
